# timers.zd — state the clock writes (#19, the timer and frame-loop half).
#
# THE QUESTION THIS FILE ANSWERS. A timer is a callback, and the language's
# claim is that state is declarative and there is no callback. So how does
# something that happens sixty times a second get into a dataflow graph
# without an imperative escape hatch?
#
# The answer is that it does not enter as control flow at all. `every` and
# `after` declare a **source signal whose writer is the browser's scheduler
# rather than a handler**. There is no block, no body and nothing to run:
# the clause says what the cell holds at each instant, and everything after
# that is the `from` and the bindings the language already had. A clock is a
# text box that types by itself.
#
# What each clause holds:
#
#   every "<duration>"   Decimal — milliseconds since this signal started
#   every frame          Decimal — the same, written once per repaint
#   after "<duration>"   Truth   — `no`, then `yes`, once
#
# THE LINE THIS MOVES, AND WHY. The tree example used to state the old
# position plainly: "a signal that changes sixty times a second is not
# state, it is an animation, and the language draws that line deliberately."
# That was the right line to draw while the only way to animate was to
# write a signal from a callback sixty times a second and recompute
# everything downstream of it. It is the wrong line now, for a reason that
# is measurable rather than aesthetic: this runtime is fine-grained, so a
# write reaches exactly the bindings that read it. `motion` below is read by
# one `Progress`, so a frame costs one attribute write — not a re-render,
# not a diff, not a pass over the view. The line moves to where the cost
# actually is.
#
# WHAT IS STILL DELIBERATELY ABSENT. Nothing here writes another signal, and
# nothing can: the clock owns the cell and the checker refuses `set` on it.
# A tick therefore cannot start a request, append to a list, or reach the
# store. If a program needs an animation to *cause* something, it says so
# with `from` and the cause is visible in the declaration rather than buried
# in a callback.
#
# `zdc dev examples/timers.zd` and watch the second row move.

# --- the three clocks ----------------------------------------------------

# A stopwatch. Ten writes a second is plenty for a display that shows
# seconds, and the value is elapsed time rather than a tick count so that
# nothing downstream has to multiply by the interval to get back what the
# timer already knew.
state elapsed is client Decimal every "100ms"

# The frame loop. Not `every "16ms"`: a 120 Hz display does not have 16 ms
# frames, and a number close to one is a lie about what the machine will do.
state motion is client Decimal every frame

# The one-shot. `setTimeout`'s whole job, as a value rather than as a call.
state greeted is client Truth after "2s"

# --- what the program derives from them ----------------------------------

state wholeSeconds is client Whole from valueOr with maybe is (
    quotient with value is elapsed,
    divisor is 1000),
    fallback is 0

# The frame count, floored so the sweep below is arithmetic over `Whole`.
state frameCount is client Whole from valueOr with maybe is (
    quotient with value is motion,
    divisor is 1),
    fallback is 0

# A two-second sweep, and the only line in this file that is about
# animation at all. `mod` is the prelude's floored remainder, so this
# restarts cleanly rather than jumping when the count wraps.
state sweep is client Whole from valueOr with maybe is (
    mod with value is frameCount,
    divisor is 2000),
    fallback is 0

# --- a component that owns its own clock ---------------------------------
#
# THE CLEANUP CASE, and the reason it is a component rather than a
# top-level signal. A component instance is built inside an owned scope, so
# hiding it disposes the scope and the disposer the clock registered runs
# with it. A timer that outlived its view would be a leak with no symptom —
# nothing renders wrongly, a callback simply keeps running — which is why
# `runtime/clock.test.js` proves it against a scheduler it can count rather
# than by looking at the page.
component Pulse with label
    state beat is client Decimal every "500ms"
    state beatCount is client Whole from valueOr with maybe is (
        quotient with value is beat,
        divisor is 500),
        fallback is 0

    Row
        Text label
        Text ": "
        Text beatCount
        Text " beats"

state showing is client Truth starting yes

view title is "Timers and frames"
    Column
        Heading "State the clock writes"

        Row
            Text "elapsed: "
            Text wholeSeconds
            Text "s"

        # One binding reads `motion`, so one frame costs one attribute
        # write. The whole argument for moving the line is this row.
        Progress sweep, most is 2000, label is "a two-second sweep, one frame at a time"

        if greeted
            Row
                Text "…and hello, two seconds late."

        Divider

        Button "show or hide the component"
            on click
                set showing to not showing

        # Hiding this disposes the instance, and disposing the instance
        # stops its timer.
        if showing
            Pulse "component clock"
